r otherwise leave the the locomotive or  or are incapacitated right after turning  off the alert ER you have 60 full  seconds before the train will start to  break and then because it's not  the most aggressive braking system it  can take depending on the speed of the  train at the time that the automatic  braking engages anywhere from thirty to  sixty additional seconds for the train  to stop so we're talking about even with  the system working the way it's designed  ninety to a hundred and twenty seconds  of potentially unsupervised train  operation this gets to the broader  problem with American trains and you've  heard me talk about this many times  before number one we have this general  issue in the US where because of the  fossil fuel lobby the airline lobby and  the automobile lobby it is very  difficult to make progress on train  development we recently saw  Massachusetts based bus company Peter  Pan actively lobbied to block a study  into train service across Massachusetts  why well that's their bread and butter  they run something like 10 or so buses a  day back and forth across the  Massachusetts Turnpike if all of a  sudden you've got train service for that  route not subject to surface traffic  quite common on i-90 the Massachusetts  Turnpike it'll be bad bad for the bus  companies and this applies to short  airline flights this applies to people  driving etc in addition to that because  we also have lacked this advancement in  train development we are missing some of  the systems that other trains in other  parts of the world have for example  forget about the 60-second alerter  the maximum speed at any given point on  the track is controlled by a computer  meaning that aside from this alerter  there would be no way if we had a system  that said once you are within I don't  know what distance it would be call it  half a mile of Hoboken station the train  can't be going 30 miles per hour by that  point the computer has already slowed  the train down to